<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Modify this file to customize your launch splash screen -->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:drawable="?android:colorBackground" />

    <!-- THE BRAND MARK, CENTRED — and it was a COMMENT until 2026-09-09.
         What `flutter create` writes is exactly this <bitmap>, wrapped in an XML
         comment under the words "You can insert your own image assets here", and
         no `launch_image` drawable anywhere in the tree for it to have named. So
         the launch window was a bare colour layer: a blank screen for the whole
         of the engine's cold start, on the one surface a user sees every single
         time they open the app.

         The colour layer above is LEFT EXACTLY AS IT WAS, deliberately. It
         follows the system theme, and swapping it for a fixed brand colour would
         make the splash the one surface in this app that ignores dark mode. The
         mark carries its own background (the brand tile — see
         assets/icon/app_icon_background.svg), so it reads on either.

         @drawable, not @mipmap: mipmap is for launcher icons, which the OS reads
         at a density other than the device's. Nothing does that with a splash.

         DERIVED, NOT PLACED: the PNGs are written by
         `node tooling/store/render-splash.mjs`, with its app flag set to subscriptiontracker, from
         assets/icon/app_icon_1024.png, and assert-launcher-icons.mjs limb 8
         re-derives them and compares decoded pixels. Do not hand-edit them.

         The flag above is named in words ON PURPOSE. XML 1.0 forbids a
         double hyphen anywhere inside a comment, and the Android resource
         compiler refuses the whole build over one: the literal flag here
         failed assembleRelease in build-platforms run 34468887825. -->
    <item>
        <bitmap
            android:gravity="center"
            android:src="@drawable/launch_image" />
    </item>
</layer-list>
